Package: terravai
Type: Package
Title: Spatial Value of Information from AI Predictions
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("Sadikul", "Islam", email = "sadikul.islamiasri@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: Turns the predictive distribution of any spatial model, from
    kriging and Bayesian models to artificial intelligence (AI) and machine
    learning models such as quantile regression forests, deep ensembles and
    conformal predictors, into decisions for threshold problems such as
    liming, fertiliser targeting or declaring a well unsafe. Provides
    decision, expected-loss, regret and break-even cost-ratio maps;
    value-of-information maps, in closed form under Gaussian updating or by
    an ensemble Kalman preposterior update that keeps the non-Gaussian shape
    of machine-learning ensembles; greedy budget-constrained sampling designs
    with a value-based stopping rule; decision-based comparison of competing
    maps, including ranking reversals between accuracy and decision regret;
    and a scale operator that carries every computation from pixels to
    districts and watersheds. Machine-learning predictive quantiles are
    joined across space with a spatial Gaussian copula. Also includes a
    dependency-free kriging engine and plain-language summaries of decision
    maps. Methods follow decision-theoretic value of information (Raiffa and
    Schlaifer, 1961, "Applied Statistical Decision Theory"), threshold-based
    elementary scores (Ehm et al., 2016, <doi:10.1111/rssb.12154>), quantile
    regression forests (Meinshausen, 2006,
    <https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v7/meinshausen06a.html>) and the ensemble
    Kalman filter (Evensen, 1994, <doi:10.1029/94JC00572>). The name
    'TerravAI' stands for Threshold Expected-Regret and Risk Analysis with
    Value of information and Artificial Intelligence.
